<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Deliberately empty, and it has to exist.

  The icon is a photograph now — the same board the main menu stands on — and a photograph has
  to be the *background* layer. A launcher parallaxes the foreground across the background as
  the user swipes, so a full-bleed picture in the foreground slides off its own edges and shows
  whatever is underneath as a bar down one side. Put it in the background and the picture is
  fixed inside the mask, which is what a photograph needs.

  That leaves nothing for the foreground to hold, and it still cannot be deleted:
  AdaptiveIconDrawable hands its foreground out to callers that do not expect null, so the layer
  is present and transparent rather than absent.

  The transparent path below is not decoration either. This file was genuinely empty for one
  build, and every launcher on the phone fell back to Android's own grid-and-robot placeholder:
  VectorDrawable.inflateChildElements throws `no path defined in <vector>` when it reaches the
  closing tag without having seen one, the exception takes the whole AdaptiveIconDrawable down
  with it, and the picture in the background layer is never reached. An empty vector is not an
  empty drawable — it is a broken one.

  The drawn pawn-in-a-corridor that used to live here is not gone — see ic_launcher_monochrome,
  which is where a flat silhouette is the only thing that can go, and ic_notification, which is
  the same idea at the height of a status bar.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="108dp"
    android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108"
    android:viewportHeight="108">
    <path
        android:fillColor="#00000000"
        android:pathData="M0,0h108v108h-108z" />
</vector>
